<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.2: Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00605</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.2: Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>25a0b83e-fea7-4bb8-b928-ff14c92902a6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Here are three types of situations. Answer the questions about each situation.</para>
      <section>
      <title>
        <emphasis>Bank Accounts</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>A customer opens a checking account and a savings account at a bank. A maximum of
        $600 will be deposited into the two accounts, some in the checking account and some in the
        savings account. (They might not deposit all of it and keep some of the money as cash.)</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x+y \leq 600\), where \(x\) represents the amount deposited in the checking
            account and \(y\) the amount deposited in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the \(x\) and \(y\) values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter \(\{0 \leq x \leq 600\}\{0 \leq y \leq 600\}\) after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media alt="Graph of inequality.  ">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_01.svg" />
              </media>
           </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((500, 100)\). $500 is deposited in checking and $100 in savings. The total is
            $600, which is the maximum amount the customer plans to deposit.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If the customer deposits $200 in their checking account, what can you say about the
            amount they deposit in their savings account?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The customer deposits $400 or less in savings.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para>For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>A customer opens a checking account and a savings account at a bank. The bank
        requires a minimum balance of $50 in the savings account. It does not matter how much money
        is kept in the checking account.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(y \geq 50\), where \(y\) is the balance in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the \(x\) values to the constraints of the
            problem (the checking account should never have less than $0), enter \(\{x \geq 0\}\)
            after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media alt="Graph of inequality.  ">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_02.svg" />
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((50, 50)\). The customer keeps $50 in savings and $50 in checking, which meets the
            minimum of $50 deposited in savings.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If the customer deposits no money in the checking account but is able to maintain
            both accounts without penalty, what can you say about the amount deposited in the
            savings account?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The customer deposits at least $50 in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      </section>
      <section>
      <title>
        <emphasis>Concert Tickets</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>Two kinds of tickets to an outdoor concert were sold: lawn tickets and seat tickets.
        Fewer than 400 tickets in total were sold.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x+y&lt;400\), where \(x\) represents the number of lawn tickets and \(y\)
            represents the number of seat tickets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the \(x\) and \(y\) values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter \(\{0 \leq x \}\{0 \leq y \}\) after your equation (because you
            cannot have a negative number of tickets).</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media alt="Graph of inequality.  ">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_03.svg" />
              </media>
           </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((100, 200)\). 100 lawn tickets and 200 seat tickets were sold. The total is 300
            tickets, which is fewer than 400.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If you know that exactly 100 lawn tickets were sold, what can you say about the
            number of seat tickets?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Fewer than 300 seat tickets were sold.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para>For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>Lawn tickets cost $30 each and seat tickets cost $50 each. The organizers made at
        least $14,000 from ticket sales.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \(30x+50y \geq 14,000\),
            where \(x\) represents the number of lawn tickets and \(y\) represents the number of
            seat tickets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the \(x\) and \(y\) values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter \(\{0 \geq x \}\{0 \geq y \}\) after your equation (because you
            cannot have a negative number of tickets).</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media
                alt="Graph of inequality. Horizontal axis, number of lawn tickets, from 0 to 500 by 50s. Vertical axis, number of seat tickets, from 0 to 500 by 50s. Slanted, solid line. Shading above.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_04.svg" />
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((100, 250)\). 100 lawn tickets and 250 seat tickets. The ticket sales would be
            $15,500, which is more than $14,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If you know that exactly 200 seat tickets were sold, what can you say about the
            number of lawn tickets?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>At least 134 lawn tickets must be sold to make at least $14,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      </section>
      <section>
      <title>
        <emphasis>Advertising Packages</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>An advertising agency offers two packages for small businesses who need advertising
        services. A basic package includes only design services. A premium package includes design
        and promotion. The agency's goal is to sell at least 60 packages in total.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x+y \geq 60\), where \(x\) represents the number of basic packages
            and \(y\) represents the number of premium packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the x and y values to the constraints of the
            problem, enter \(\{x \geq 0\}\{y \geq 0\}\) after your equation (because the business will
            not have a negative number of packages).</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/41e534468511ee366a55eaf635a19ab9ff1afd95.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media
                alt="Graph of inequality. Horizontal axis, number of basic packages, from 0 to 100 by 10s. Vertical axis, number of premium packages, from 0 to 100 by 10s. Slanted, solid line. Shading above.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_05.svg" />
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((50, 30)\). 50 basic packages and 30 premium ones are sold. The total is 80, which
            is more than 60.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If the agency sells exactly 45 basic packages, what can you say about the number of
            premium packages it needs to sell to meet its goal?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The agency needs to sell at least 15 premium packages to meet its goal.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para>For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>The basic advertising package has a value of $1000 and the premium package has a
        value of $2500. The goal of the agency is to sell more than $60,000 worth of small-business
        advertising packages.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(1,000x+2,500y&gt;60,000\), where \(x\) represents the number of basic packages
            and \(y\) represents the number of premium packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para>Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the \(x\) and \(y\) values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter \(\{x \geq 0\}\{y \geq 0\}\) after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/41e534468511ee366a55eaf635a19ab9ff1afd95.html" width="500"
          height="400" />
        </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <figure class="unnumbered">
            <media
              alt="Graph of inequality. Horizontal axis, number of basic packages, from 0 to 100 by 10s. Vertical axis, number of premium packages, from 0 to 100 by 10s. Slanted, dotted line. Shading above.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_06.svg" />
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>\((10, 30)\). 10 basic packages and 30 premium packages are sold. The total value is
            $85,000, which is more than $60,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>If you know that exactly 10 premium packages were sold, what can you say about the
            number of basic packages the agency needs to sell to meet its goal?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The agency needs to sell more than 35 basic packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <section>
      <title>Video: Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about solving inequalities in two variables.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4"
          src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/677d4998c3993ec07f8d8d2259047887e38ec5b3" />
      </media>
